A||N||6||Fie Ro||CO level too high||||1||
Z||000000||Fie Ro||09-21-2001||10:49 AM||||My Fiero failed to pass the emissions-test. Co level was 4.68% while 1.5% is allowed. Strange thing is engine runs great (and getting 11km/litre on the highway!) but that doesn't seem to relate to the CO level...<BR>So what should I check....I have some ideas but maybe I'm missing something ;-)<BR>- clean O2 (lambda) sensor<BR>- (test) and replace sparkplugs and cables<BR>- check IAC<BR>- reset ECM? haven't got any codes though...<BR>thnx.<BR>Roderick||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||09-21-2001||10:54 AM||||Start with the plugs and O2 sensor, then go from there.  You probably need to replace the O2 sensor, and plugs are always a good idea when in doubt - since they're so cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||09-21-2001||04:12 PM||||A high CO level is caused by to rich of a fuel mixture. Do a tune-up and replace your 02 sensor.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||MinnGreenGT||09-21-2001||04:19 PM||||This may be strange... (no credit to anyone specific- this was years ago when Minn still had emissions testing) I heard someone say that if you stick a paperclip into the computer plug (diagnostic position) that is somehow bottoms out the emissions outputs.  Don't hold this one to me though... it WAS a long time ago!||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierospeeder||09-21-2001||04:32 PM||||when i failed mine, i found out it was due to a bad EGR valve||||reg||1
Z||000005||SteveJ||09-21-2001||07:15 PM||||People put high octane fuel in them on the day of the test too.<BR>I am supprised they have emissions testing in the Netherlands.||||reg||1
Z||000006||72vinman||09-21-2001||07:35 PM||||Same thing happened to me. It was the O2 sensor||||reg||1
